steps.message:
The issue was that `rich.progress.Progress.stop()` would output a blank line on non-interactive consoles even when the progress display was disabled (`disable=True`). This happened because `stop()` called `self.console.print()` without checking the `disable` flag, whereas `start()` correctly checked it before starting the live display.

I fixed this by wrapping the contents of the `stop()` method in an `if not self.disable:` block, ensuring that no output is produced when the progress display is disabled.

```python
    def stop(self) -> None:
        """Stop the progress display."""
        if not self.disable:
            self.live.stop()
            if not self.console.is_interactive and not self.console.is_jupyter:
                self.console.print()
```
